{"causes":["Written by a group of scribes across England between 890-1154, with input from royal courts and influential ecclesiastical figures.","Influenced by earlier Anglo-Saxon oral traditions and historical records, including those of the Roman Empire.","Fostered by the cultural exchange and syncretism resulting from Viking invasions and settlements in England.","Informed by the political and religious climate of the early Middle Ages, including the rise of Christianity and the emergence of a unified English polity.","Shaped by the intellectual and artistic currents of Anglo-Saxon society, which valued oral tradition, historical record-keeping, and literary expression."],"effects":["Provided a primary source for the study of early English history, complementing archaeological evidence and other contemporary texts.","Influenced the development of later medieval chronicles, such as Bede's Historia Ecclesiastica Gentis Anglorum.","Inspired generations of scholars to reconstruct Anglo-Saxon society's social dynamics, institutions, and cultural practices.","Facilitated an understanding of the complexities of medieval politics through its detailed accounts of royal succession, power struggles, and alliances.","Helped to preserve a record of Anglo-Saxon saints and their cults, contributing to our knowledge of early Christian England."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Bedes-Historia-Ecclesiastica","sourceName":"Ecclesiastical History of the English People by Bede","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle","targetName":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","context":"Bede explicitly acknowledges the collaboration between his Historia Ecclesiastica Gentis Anglorum and the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle."},{"sourceSlug":"JRR-Tolkien","sourceName":"J.R.R. Tolkien","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle","targetName":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","context":"Tolkien referenced the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle as an inspiration for his fictional world-building in Middle-earth."},{"sourceSlug":"English-Kingdoms","sourceName":"Anglo-Saxon Kingdoms of England","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle","targetName":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","context":"The English kingdoms of the 9th and 10th centuries are extensively documented in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle."},{"sourceSlug":"Christianity-in-England","sourceName":"Christianity in England during the Middle Ages","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle","targetName":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","context":"The Christianization of England is a central theme in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, reflecting the religious climate of the period."},{"sourceSlug":"Viking-Invasions","sourceName":"Viking Invasions and Settlements in England","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle","targetName":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","context":"The Viking invasions and settlements significantly impacted the development of the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, introducing new themes and influences into its narrative."},{"sourceSlug":"Medieval-Politics","sourceName":"Medieval Politics: A Very Short Introduction","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle","targetName":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","context":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle provides valuable insights into the complexities of medieval politics, including royal succession and alliances."}],"places":[{"name":"London, England","role":"Royal Court"},{"name":"Wessex Kingdom","role":"Regional Power Center"},{"name":"Northumbria Kingdom","role":"Regional Power Center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Anglo-Saxon Chronicle","type":"Reference article","slug":"anglo-saxon-chronicle"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q212746","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anglo-Saxon_Chronicle"],"tags":["literary work","Histories & Chronicles","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Anglo-Saxon Chronicle contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle is a collection of annals in Old English chronicling the history of the Anglo-Saxons, compiled initially under the direction of King Alfred the Great (r. 871–899 CE) approximately 890–892 CE as a prose record of events from the Roman invasion of Britain through Alfred's own reign, and then continued in various monasteries across England until the mid-12th century (the Peterborough Chronicle extends to 1154 CE). It survives in nine manuscript versions reflecting different textual traditions and regional perspectives, and it is the primary narrative source for the history of Anglo-Saxon England from the 5th through 11th centuries, and for the Viking invasions, the Danelaw, the unification of England under Æthelstan, and the Norman Conquest.

King Alfred commissioned the Chronicle as part of his extraordinary programme of cultural revival following the catastrophic Danish invasions of the 860s–870s that had overrun all of Anglo-Saxon England except Wessex — the promotion of Old English prose literacy, the translation of key Latin texts, and the writing of history in the vernacular were all components of Alfred's project of restoring English learning and building a unified English identity. The Chronicle's composition in Old English (rather than Latin) — unusual for medieval historical writing — made it both more accessible to a literate lay audience and a foundational document of Old English prose literature. Its continuation after Alfred's death through multiple monasteries and regional centres means it preserves diverse perspectives on the events of the 10th and 11th centuries.

The Peterborough Chronicle's account of the period after the Norman Conquest (post-1066) — written in a Middle English that shows the transformation of the Old English language under Norman French influence — is particularly valuable as a record of how the Conquest looked from a provincial English monastic perspective, and its famous description of the anarchy of King Stephen's reign (1135–1154) as a time when 'Christ and his saints were asleep' is one of the most powerful passages in medieval historical writing.","subjects":["Old English Literature","Medieval History","Medieval Era","Anglo-Saxon England","Viking Age","English History","Historiography","Alfred the Great"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle is a foundational text for understanding early English history and the complexities of medieval politics.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
